      "sectionCode": "book6-article-019-body",
      "heading": "Reading the Book VI structure",
      "anchor": "article-body",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The internet made continuous information exchange a shared infrastructure, global supply chains turned locations in different countries into nodes of the same production process, and global cities concentrated high-density coordination among firms, finance, logistics and talent."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "These layers depend on one another. Digital connectivity cannot replace physical logistics, and supply chains require cities and professional services for coordination. As efficiency rises, redundancy, substitutes and recoverability become more important."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This article remains within the completed Book VI manuscript; B6-WEB-B does not add live current-data population. Subjective experience and structural conditions remain distinct, and no single measure is used to infer a person or an entire society."
        }
      ]
    }
